{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Firefly Sea: Bioluminescent Bacteria of the Puerto Rico Bioluminescent Bay",
    "date": "2026-09-14",
    "lesson": "Mosquito Bay in Vieques, Puerto Rico, glows at night because of a dense standing population of the dinoflagellate Pyrodinium bahamense, whose light-flashes (a predator-startling defense) are amplified by symbiotic and free-living luminous bacteria (Vibrio-type) living in the same warm, mangrove-fringed, restricted-circulation lagoon. Red mangrove leaf litter leaches tannins and carbohydrate-rich detritus that feed a copiotrophic bacterial community, while the narrow channel to the sea keeps nutrients trapped and the water warm and still. This level asks you to survive as one of those bacteria: carbohydrate-dominated leaf-litter detritus is your main food, night is not a famine (the bay stays productive because it is so enclosed and shallow), but grazing dinoflagellates and their sudden luminous flashes make the water a place where being caught is highly visible. Chitin fragments from tiny crustaceans add a protein pulse. The lesson: bioluminescence is fundamentally an ecological arms race over visibility, and a shallow, poorly-flushed tropical lagoon can concentrate both nutrients and life to extraordinary densities compared to the open sea.",
    "citation": "Seliger et al. 1971, 'Bioluminescence and phytoplankton successions in Bahia Fosforescente'; Vieques Mosquito Bay ecology studies",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Mosquito Bay / Bahía Fosforescente, Vieques, Puerto Rico - bioluminescent lagoon ecosystem"
  },
